--- final message ---
I wrote `paper_strategy.json` and `paper_strategy.md`, with status `ready`. I appended the framing decision to `memory.md`, `global_memory/memory.md` and `global_memory/memory.jsonl`. The JSON parses. I did not run the final paper validation, as instructed.

**The paper this supports:** a measurement and limitations paper, not a new-method paper. The selected method is the original first-order influence search, which was kept unchanged. The three attempted improved variants were all unscored, so they count as missing evidence, not losses. The search stage shows a baseline score of 0.914 with a delta of 0.0. The final evaluation completed and the outcome is `qualified`.

**Central thesis:** the influence search succeeds very differently across evaluation designs. All numbers below come from the sealed final evaluation.

| Design | Edit success score | Rank 1 displaced at 2% budget (drop / flip) |
|---|---|---|
| Crowd (Arena) | 1.000 | yes / yes |
| Sports (NBA) | 0.833 | yes / yes |
| Expert human (MT-Bench) | 0.374 | no / yes |
| LLM judge (MT-Bench) | 0.216 | no / no |

The equally weighted mean is 0.606. The constant and random baselines score 0.071 each and the win-rate heuristic scores 0.574. The margin versus the best baseline is 0 because the studied method is the influence baseline itself. The permuted-input control gap is 0 and the valid proposal rate is 1.0. The three seeds give identical scores, so there is no between-seed variance and no confidence interval to report.

**Framings rejected:**
- **Method improvement:** nothing was scored.
- **Noise versus intent with calibration and subsampling ablations:** the artifacts contain no such experiments.

**Figures and layout:**
- **Overview diagram:** a concept figure placed on page 1–2.
- **Bar figure:** two panels, with design scores on the left and the baseline scale on the right.
- **Tables:** one for the evaluation setup and one for the displacement rates.
- **Placement:** all figures are planned at full text width, since the ICLR template is single column.

The supplement covers the evaluator specification, the full metric table, the seeded-run evidence, the unscored variants, the algorithm description and the LLM usage statement. I also left the writer notes on honesty limits, the ICLR body-page plan, and forbidden headings and phrases.

**Gaps the writer needs to know about:**
- **Missing citations:** `references.bib` contains only Bradley-Terry theory papers. It lacks Chatbot Arena, MT-Bench, influence functions, the robustness metric paper and the vote-rigging paper, so the writer is told to cite only what exists.
- **Sparse evidence:** the final metrics give no per-case, per-budget or per-design baseline numbers, so the writer is told not to invent any.
- **Page count:** with this little evidence, the 8–9 page body will be hard to fill without padding. I told the writer to tighten rather than pad.
